<!-- ◆ SIMPLE PARAGRAPH -->
<div class="ename"><div class="wrap"><span class="en">◆ SIMPLE PARAGRAPH</span><span class="eu">the workhorse text block · short headline left, body prose right, first line is the lede size · pull .paper .story</span></div></div>
<section class="paper"><div class="wrap story">
  <div class="side rv">
    <span class="k mono">\ The number</span>
    <h2>A community, turned into a <span class="serif">creative business.</span></h2>
  </div>
  <div class="body rv">
    <p>When I joined Art of Visuals, it was a community page with a massive audience, pushing two million, sharing inspiration, reposting, and running giveaways. Every piece of work lived on AOV's own channels.</p>
    <p>Four years later it was a working agency: commercials with budgets north of $200K, influencer campaigns with the biggest voices in tech, an MSI campaign that launched in Times Square, and a global client roster. Revenue was 4x.</p>
    <p>The audience was mostly there before me. Some of the brands were too. What changed was how we engaged them. The agency is what we built, and the creative was my lane.</p>
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- ◆ TWO-COLUMN · light + dark -->
<div class="ename"><div class="wrap"><span class="en">◆ TWO-COLUMN · light + dark</span><span class="eu">a simple text block, body in two columns · works on paper or ink · pull .block .cols (add .block-dark on a dark ground)</span></div></div>
<section class="paper"><div class="wrap block">
  <span class="k mono">\ How it happened · on light</span>
  <h2>Everything culture <span class="serif">touches.</span></h2>
  <div class="cols rv">
    <p><b>The offer:</b> retainers, campaigns, one-off ads, distribution on AOV's channels, strategy and consulting. Weekly content that lived on brands' channels and in their ad accounts, focused on the outdoor space and consumer electronics.</p>
    <p><b>The wins:</b> Sony, Adorama, MSI, Intel, Lexar, Peak Design, WANDRD. Clients from LA and New York to Japan and China. By the end: $200K+ commercials, and an MSI campaign launching in Times Square.</p>
    <p><b>The team:</b> direct reports across the video team, scaled with crew and designers per production. I ran all creative on the agency side and carried the pitches, the sales, and the client relationships.</p>
    <p><b>The community:</b> it never stopped being one. Workshops, meetups, a weekly podcast at 30K monthly listeners. That is exactly what made the agency credible.</p>
  </div>
</div></section>
<section class="block-dark"><div class="wrap block">
  <span class="k mono">\ The system · on dark</span>
  <h2>One brand, the whole <span class="serif">studio.</span></h2>
  <div class="cols rv">
    <p>Flashpoint was a flash brand. Good gear, better than its price suggested, but the brand was pointed at one shelf. Adorama's own sales data told a bigger story: people were buying Flashpoint for everything.</p>
    <p>So the rebrand's job wasn't a fresh coat of paint. It was permission to expand: an identity that could live on a flash, a light stand, and a shipping box.</p>
    <p>Identity, packaging, product, messaging, web, and out-of-home. Six disciplines, one voice. We built the brand book and the standards, then designed hundreds of packages against them.</p>
    <p>Then we handed it over. Templates, standards, and a line Adorama could run themselves. The point was to build something that kept working after we left.</p>
  </div>
</div></section>

